> "for thou art with me;  
> thy rod and thy staff they comfort me."  
> (Psalm 23:4)

> "With thee is heaven, where'er the spot;  
> Each place is hell where thou art not."  
> — Book II, Canto XXX, ll. 43–44 (translated by Ralph T. H. Griffith)

> "Make haste to enjoy life before death; for you do not know how long you will remain;  
> I see that the graves are full of the young who were cut short."  
> (translated by A. J. Arberry, p. 77)

> My mistress' eyes are nothing like the sun;  
> Coral is far more red than her lips' red;  
> If snow be white, why then her breasts are dun;  
> If hairs be wires, black wires grow on her head.  
> (Rolfe, ed., Sonnet 130, ll. 1–4)

> For God's sake hold your tongue, and let me love;  
> Or chide my palsy, or my gout;  
> My five gray hairs, or ruin'd fortune flout;  
> With wealth your state, your mind with arts improve;  
> Take you a course, get you a place,  
> Observe his Honour, or his Grace;  
> Or the king's real, or his stamp'd face  
> Contemplate; what you will, approve,  
> So you will let me love.  
> (Grierson, ed., "The Canonization", ll. 1–9)

> We'll build in sonnets pretty rooms;  
> As well a well-wrought urn becomes  
> The greatest ashes, as half-acre tombs,  
> And by these hymns, all shall approve  
> Us canonized for love;  
> (Grierson, ed., "The Canonization", ll. 32–36)

> And thus invoke us, 'You, whom reverend love  
> Made one another's hermitage;  
> You, to whom love was peace, that now is rage;  
> Who did the whole world's soul contract, and drove  
> Into the glasses of your eyes;  
> So made such mirrors, and such spies,  
> That they did all to you epitomize—  
> Countries, towns, courts beg from above  
> A pattern of your love.'  
> (Grierson, ed., "The Canonization", ll. 37–45)

> Moving of th' earth brings harms and fears;  
> Men reckon what it did, and meant;  
> But trepidation of the spheres,  
> Though greater far, is innocent.  
> (John Donne, *The Complete English Poems*, ed. A. J. Smith, ll. 9–12)

> Together let us beat this ample field,  
> Try what the open, what the covert yield;  
> \[...\]  
> Laugh where we must, be candid where we can;  
> But vindicate the ways of God to man.  
> (Mack, ed., *An Essay on Man*, Epistle I, ll. 9–16)

> All nature is but art, unknown to thee;  
> All chance, direction, which thou canst not see;  
> All discord, harmony not understood;  
> All partial evil, universal good:  
> And, spite of pride, in erring reason's spite,  
> One truth is clear, Whatever is, is right.  
> (Mack, ed., *An Essay on Man*, Epistle I, ll. 289–294)

> I hold it true, whate'er befall;  
> I feel it, when I sorrow most;  
> 'Tis better to have loved and lost  
> Than never to have loved at all.  
> (Tennyson, *In Memoriam A.H.H.*, Section XXVII, ll. 13–16)

> One stiff blind horse, his every bone a-stare,  
> Stood stupefied, however he came there:  
> Thrust out past service from the devil's stud!  
> Alive? he might be dead for aught I know,  
> With that red gaunt and colloped neck a-strain,  
> And shut eyes underneath the rusty mane;  
> Seldom went such grotesqueness with such woe;  
> I never saw a brute I hated so;  
> He must be wicked to deserve such pain.  
> (Browning, "Childe Roland to the Dark Tower Came", stanzas XIII–XIV, ll. 73–84)

> Ah, take the Cash in hand and waive the Rest;  
> Oh, the brave Music of a distant Drum!  
> (FitzGerald, *Rubaiyat of Omar Khayyam*, stanza XII)

> Where the mind is without fear and the head is held high;  
> Where knowledge is free;  
> (Tagore, *Gitanjali*, Song 35, opening lines; Macmillan, 1912/1913)

> Where the world has not been broken up into fragments by narrow  
> domestic walls;  
> Where words come out from the depth of truth;  
> (Tagore, *Gitanjali*, Song 35; Macmillan, 1912/1913)

> Where tireless striving stretches its arms towards perfection;  
> Where the clear stream of reason has not lost its way into the dreary  
> desert sand of dead habit;  
> (Tagore, *Gitanjali*, Song 35; Macmillan, 1912/1913)

> I fled Him, down the nights and down the days;  
> I fled Him, down the arches of the years;  
> I fled Him, down the labyrinthine ways  
> Of my own mind; and in the mist of tears  
> I hid from Him, and under running laughter.  
> (Thompson, "The Hound of Heaven", Section I, ll. 1–5)

> No! I am not Prince Hamlet, nor was meant to be;  
> Am an attendant lord, one that will do  
> To swell a progress, start a scene or two,  
> Advise the prince; no doubt, an easy tool,  
> Deferential, glad to be of use,  
> Politic, cautious, and meticulous;  
> Full of high sentence, but a bit obtuse;  
> At times, indeed, almost ridiculous—  
> Almost, at times, the Fool.  
> (Eliot, "The Love Song of J. Alfred Prufrock", ll. 111–119; *Prufrock and Other Observations*, 1920)

> Turning and turning in the widening gyre  
> The falcon cannot hear the falconer;  
> Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold;  
> Mere anarchy is loosed upon the world,  
> The blood-dimmed tide is loosed, and everywhere  
> The ceremony of innocence is drowned;  
> (Yeats, "The Second Coming", ll. 1–6; Finneran, ed., *The Collected Poems of W. B. Yeats*, 1989)
